\page swift_tex Swift
 
{#section:swift}

![image](61_pidt/ExCALIBUR.png){width="20%"}

 is a SWIFT (SPH With Inter-dependent Fine-grained Tasking) clone which
combines the SWIFT SPH kernels (tasks) with Peano's
particle-in-dual-tree concept. The original SWIFT codes is available
from <https://swift.dur.ac.uk>. 's particle handling is a follow-up to
the 2015 paper "Two Particle-in-Grid Realisations on Spacetrees"
(<https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.02435>). It is discussed in detail in
Section [\[section:pidt\]](#section:pidt){reference-type="ref"
reference="section:pidt"}.

The integration of the SWIFT kernels and  has been made possible by the
EPSRC ExCALIBUR grants Massively Parallel Particle Hydrodynamics for
Engineering and Astrophysics (EP/V001523/1) and PAX-SPH.  is realised as
Python layer on top of  and brings 's core, its particle toolsbox and
SWIFT together.

##### A first  pidt run (native usage of particle interface) {#a-first-pidt-run-native-usage-of-particle-interface .unnumbered}

-   Configure  with the flags `–enable-particles`:

    libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp src/config.h.in .;
    automake --add-missing ./configure --enable-particles

    These are the two mandatory extensions. You might want to add
    further options.

-   Recompile your  code. This should give you a couple of
    `ToolboxParticles` libraries in the `src/toolbox/particles`
    subdirectory.

-   Ensure that `python` subdirectory is in your Python search path and

-   Yet to be written

Set up a simulation
-------------------

Our interface for  is almost complete realised in Python. We configure
the whole simulation in Python, but typically make the key steps of a
simulation point to generic C++ routines which are shipped with .
Alterantively, we can inject C++ code manually into the Python code
(which is great for prototyping). Once the Python script is executed, it
generates a plain C++ code with a makefile which waves 's core and the
SPH kernels together.

We start with the creation of a  project:

-   global parameters

-   generate files

-   compile

Introduce a new particle
------------------------

The generation of a particle is straightforward. We employ a tool called
DaStGen2 which really just is a set of Python classes which represent
the attributes of a C++ class. We build up a set of Python objects and
 eventually dumps them into C++ code from which it is embedded into the
actual simulator.

Though conceptionally a plain C++ pretty printer, the DaStGen2 Python
API can create some fancy C++ annotations. You can, for example, specify
ranges in which integers live, or you can prescribe the precision you
wanna have for floating point values. If you have a specialised LLVM
compiler, these annotations will be used to generate aggressively
optimised code.

In 's *dual tree* storage scheme, particles are always stored to their
nearest neighbour.

Input and output
----------------

 dumps a lot of information to the terminal by default. Consult
Section [\[section:logging:log-filter\]](#section:logging:log-filter){reference-type="ref"
reference="section:logging:log-filter"} for some remarks on how to
filter the output properly.

For the output of the data, the code can dump the particle data in
various formats. The easiest format to use is vtu data.

High-performance computing {#section:particles:swift:hpc}
--------------------------

To use 's high-performance computing features, you have to configure
your baseline code properly. That is, you need to invoke `./configure`
with an appropriate `–with-multithreading=...` call to enable
multithreading, you need to invoke it with `–with-mpi=...` for MPI
support, and you need to specify `–with-gpus=...` if you want to use
accelerators. Furthermore, you will have to enable 's load balancing
toolbox (`–enable-loadbalancing`), unless you want to implement load
balancing all on your own.

